  "title": "Calorie estimates: remaining count units (head, bunch, \"N g each\", bare can)",
  "description": "Progress 2026-10-02: curated entries and SR food_overrides can now carry cited piece weights (see the catalog README), and the first batch landed (corn tortilla sizes, anchovy, ear of corn, bacon slice, garlic head, parsnip, ham and prosciutto slices, lettuce leaf and head, baguette, hamburger and hot dog buns, lasagna noodles, nori sheets); pipeline-fixture lines with Unsupported quantity unit went from 874 to 701, prod (owner) from 151 to 114. \
Still open: shallots (the largest group; no USDA, CNF or label source weighs one), bunches, inch pieces of ginger, poblano peppers (its FNDDS food is generic hot peppers, 15 g whole), plus the original parser and owner questions below. \
After count units landed, about 740 pipeline-fixture lines (1.2%) still fail with Unsupported quantity unit. \
USDA SR Legacy has no per-piece weight for these, so each needs a cited curated value or a parser change rather than an import rule. \
Largest groups: (1) \"head\" (garlic, lettuce, cauliflower) and \"bunch\" (herbs, kale, scallions): add curated per-piece weights with a source. (2) \"g each\" / \"oz each\" / \"lb each\" (\"2 chicken breasts, 200 g each\"): the amount is a per-item weight and the count lives elsewhere on the line; check what the parser produces before deciding. (3) A bare \"can\" / \"package\" with no stated weight: probably stays unknown, but a standard 15-ounce can for beans and tomatoes is worth an owner decision. (4) \"inch piece\" of ginger and \"handful\". (5) \"packed cup\" is a denser measure than USDA cups for herbs and greens; it currently fails and should probably stay unknown for leafy foods and use the plain density for brown sugar. \
Run make ingredient-catalog-audit to measure before and after.",
